 ],
 "literal": "The majestic fine male wild bull with ring-like horns / chews the long ears of the red-stemmed padavu grass, / and looking at the gentle-eyed wild cow, sighing with distress, / rests in the striped shade of the rough-trunked Ukā tree. / To cross the painful, difficult desert path, / is it pleasant, O Chief, having separated from your dear companion?",
 "translation": "O Chief! Is it really pleasant to traverse the painful, difficult desert path, having separated from your sweet companion, when even the majestic wild bull with ring-like horns, after chewing the long ears of the red-stemmed padavu grass, looks at its gentle-eyed mate with deep concern and rests with her in the striped shade of the rough-trunked Ukā tree?",
 "modern": "வளையம்போன்ற கொம்புகளையுடைய கம்பீரமான நல்ல காட்டு எருது, செந்நிறத் தண்டையுடைய அருகம்புல்லின் நீண்ட கதிர்களை மேய்ந்துவிட்டு, தன் மடப்பம் பொருந்திய கண்களையுடைய பெண் மானை (மரையா) நோக்கி, வெம்மை தாளாமல் வருந்தி, சொரசொரப்பான அடிமரத்தையுடைய உகாய் மரத்தின் வரிவரியான நிழலில் தங்கி இளைப்பாறும். அத்தகைய துன்பம் தரும் அரிய பாலைவன வழியைக் கடந்து செல்வது, உமது இனிய துணையைப் பிரிந்து சென்ற பின் உமக்கு இன்பத்தைத் தருமோ, தலைவனே?",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the heroine's friend to the hero when he plans to journey through the arid desert to earn wealth. She describes the harshness of the desert path, but contrasts it with a tender scene of animal companionship. Even in the scorching heat, a majestic wild bull grazes on grass and then, looking with deep affection and concern at its gentle-eyed mate, rests with her in the thin, striped shade of a rough-trunked Ukā tree. By presenting this image, the friend subtly asks the hero how he could bear to leave his loving wife behind to travel through such a wasteland. The contrast between the wild animals staying together in adversity and the human hero choosing to separate highlights the emotional pain of separation in the pālai landscape.",
